\title{The Arithmetic of Mixed-Domain Key Budgets\\
\large A model-free theory of attention knees under pooling, interleaving\\ and unequal mixing}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-09-01}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a model-free theory of the \emph{key budget} --- the number of top-ranked
attention weights required to retain a prescribed fraction $\tau$ of the total attention
mass of a context --- and of how that budget behaves when two or more content domains are
combined. Two combination operators are analysed: \emph{pooling}, in which the domains share
one context with mixing weights $a$ and $b$, and \emph{interleaving}, in which the domains
alternate along a context of doubled length. The central structural fact is that
interleaving \emph{is} pooling read in doubled key units: an even prefix of an interleaved
context contains matched prefixes of both domains. From this reduction we obtain a complete
account of the empirical verdict ``a mixed domain starts at the easier component's level and
rises at double the expected rate''. The results are: a \emph{mediant sandwich} caging the
pooled budget between the component budgets for every mixing ratio; a \emph{no-formula
theorem} showing that no function of the component budgets computes the mixed budget; an
exact \emph{convex-combination identity} with weight the mass share, and the resulting
\emph{mass-share rigidity} theorem; the \emph{doubling law}
$|\Delta_{\mix} - 2\Delta_{\pool}| \le 1$, generalised to multiplier $m$ for $m$-fold round
robins and to multiplier $s+1$ --- the reciprocal of the rarest mixing rate --- for $s{:}1$
interleaving; a \emph{gate staircase} with sharp stability radii, yielding a
signal-to-resolution conservation inequality with equality exactly for balanced mixtures; an
exact treatment of block interleaving in which the residual $\pm b$ quantisation window
collapses to a single integer; a \emph{closed formula} for the critical mixing weight of a
ratio sweep in terms of four pure-domain head masses; and \emph{two-sided spectral
estimators} turning a single observed budget into a bracket on the per-key decay ratio.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Attention mechanisms distribute, at each query position, a positive weight over the keys of
the context. Sorting those weights in decreasing order and asking how many are needed to
account for a fraction $\tau$ of their total gives a single integer --- the \emph{key
budget}, or \emph{knee}. It is the natural operational notion of a model's working memory:
keys past the knee can be pruned with controlled loss of mass.

Measuring the knee on pure program source, on pure natural-language prose, and on a stream
that interleaves blocks of each, at two context lengths, produces:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
context $n$ & interleaved & code & prose & interleaved increment\\
\midrule
$512$ & $12$ & $12$ & $16$ & ---\\
$1024$ & $20$ & $16$ & $20$ & $+8$ (each pure domain: $+4$)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three readings were proposed: (P1) the mixed knee is the midpoint of the component knees;
(P2) it reaches the harder domain's level as the context grows; (P3) mixed-domain attention
has structure of its own. This paper replaces these slogans with theorems.

\subsection{What is and is not explained}

\emph{Starts low.} At the shorter context the mixture is indistinguishable from the easier
component. This is a \emph{rigidity} phenomenon: as soon as one domain's mass share is large
enough that a certain gate window fits inside a single step of that domain's knee staircase,
the mixture's knee is \emph{exactly} that domain's knee.

\emph{Rises fast.} The doubled increment is a \emph{change of units}. Interleaving two
domains produces a context of doubled length whose even prefixes are matched prefixes of the
components; every budget is therefore expressed in half-keys. The doubling occurs even when
a domain is interleaved with a rescaled copy of itself, so it carries no information about
cross-domain interaction.

\section{Definitions}

An \emph{attention profile} is a function $w : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ with $w_i > 0$;
the intended reading is that $w_i$ is the $i$-th largest attention weight.

\begin{definition}[Head mass]
$H_w(k) = \sum_{i<k} w_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retained mass]
For $n \ge 1$ and $k \ge 0$,
$R_w(n,k) = H_w(\min(k,n))/H_w(n) \in [0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee / key budget]
For $\tau \le 1$, $k^*_w(n,\tau) = \min\{k : \tau \le R_w(n,k)\}$.
\end{definition}

Two facts are used constantly: $\tau \le R_w(n,k^*)$, and $R_w(n,k) < \tau$ for $k<k^*$.
Consequently, if $R_w(n,m) < \tau \le R_w(n,m+1)$ then $k^*_w(n,\tau) = m+1$. Knees are
nondecreasing in $n$.

\begin{definition}[Context-doubling increment]
$\Delta_w(\tau,n) = k^*_w(2n,\tau) - k^*_w(n,\tau)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pooling]
For $a,b>0$, $(\pool_{a,b}(u,v))_i = a u_i + b v_i$; head masses add.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Interleaving]
$(\mix(u,v))_i = u_{i/2}$ for even $i$ and $v_{(i-1)/2}$ for odd $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Block interleaving]
For $b \ge 1$, writing $i = b\beta + \rho$ with $\rho<b$,
$(\blockmix_b(u,v))_i = u_{b\lfloor \beta/2\rfloor + \rho}$ if $\beta$ is even and
$v_{b\lfloor \beta/2\rfloor + \rho}$ if $\beta$ is odd. Thus $\blockmix_1 = \mix$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Round robin and family pool]
$(\rr_m U)_i = U^{(i \bmod m)}_{\lfloor i/m\rfloor}$ and
$(\poolFam_m U)_i = \sum_{j<m} U^{(j)}_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Unequal-rate interleaving]
For $s \ge 1$ the $s{:}1$ pattern places $s$ keys of $u$ then one key of $v$, periodically;
its pooled partner bundles $s$ keys of $u$ with one key of $v$ per pooled key. The rates are
$p_u = s/(s+1)$, $p_v = 1/(s+1)$, so $1/\min_j p_j = s+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mass share]
$\lambda = \dfrac{a H_u(n)}{a H_u(n) + b H_v(n)} \in (0,1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Step width]
$\sw_w(n,k) = w_k / H_w(n)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The mediant sandwich and the refutation of P1}

\begin{lemma}[Mediant inequality]\label{lem:mediant}
For $B_1,B_2>0$,
$\min(A_1/B_1, A_2/B_2) \le (A_1+A_2)/(B_1+B_2) \le \max(A_1/B_1,A_2/B_2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $m$ is the minimum then $mB_1 \le A_1$ and $mB_2 \le A_2$; add and divide by $B_1+B_2$.
The upper bound is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mediant Sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For positive profiles $u,v$, weights $a,b>0$, $n \ge 1$ and every $k$,
\[
\min\bigl(R_u(n,k),R_v(n,k)\bigr) \le R_{\pool_{a,b}(u,v)}(n,k) \le \max\bigl(R_u(n,k),R_v(n,k)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Head masses add, so the pooled retained mass is the mediant of the component retained masses
with denominators $aH_u(n)$ and $bH_v(n)$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mediant}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The cage]\label{cor:cage}
For $\tau \le 1$,
$\min(k^*_u, k^*_v) \le k^*_{\pool_{a,b}(u,v)} \le \max(k^*_u,k^*_v)$, all at $(n,\tau)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At $K = \max(k^*_u,k^*_v)$ both components pass, so by the lower half of the sandwich the
mixture passes. Conversely at the mixture's own knee the mixture passes, so by the upper half
some component passes there, and that component's knee is at most the mixture's.
\end{proof}

Fix $n=4$, $\tau = 7/10$, $v = (1,1,1,1)$ (so $k^*_v = 3$) and
\[
u_A = (10,1,1,1), \quad u_B = (100,1,1,1), \quad u_C = (1/10,\ 1/1000,\ 1/1000,\ 1/1000),
\]
each with $k^*_u = 1$.

\begin{theorem}[The whole cage is attained]\label{thm:witnesses}
With $a=b=1$, $k^*_{\pool}(u_A,v) = 2$, $k^*_{\pool}(u_B,v) = 1$ and
$k^*_{\pool}(u_C,v) = 3$, while all three pairs have component knees $1$ and $3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct computation. $u_A+v = (11,2,2,2)$ gives retained masses $11/17 < 0.7 \le 13/17$, knee
$2$; $u_B+v = (101,2,2,2)$ gives $101/107 \ge 0.7$, knee $1$; $u_C+v$ is nearly flat, with
retained masses $\approx 0.268, 0.512, 0.756$, knee $3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No-Formula Theorem]
There is no $f : \mathbb{N}^2 \to \mathbb{N}$ with
$k^*_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(n,\tau) = f(k^*_u(n,\tau), k^*_v(n,\tau))$ for all positive profiles,
all $n \ge 1$ and all $\tau \le 1$. In particular P1 is false.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Such an $f$ would need $f(1,3)=2$ and $f(1,3)=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:witnesses}.
\end{proof}

\section{The convex identity and mass-share rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Convex-Combination Identity]\label{thm:convex}
With $\lambda$ the mass share,
$R_{\pool_{a,b}(u,v)}(n,k) = \lambda R_u(n,k) + (1-\lambda) R_v(n,k)$ for every $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand both sides over the common denominator $aH_u(n)+bH_v(n)$; the component denominators
cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shifted-gate bracket]\label{thm:shifted}
If $\tau/\lambda \le 1$ then
\[
k^*_u\!\left(n, \frac{\tau - (1-\lambda)}{\lambda}\right) \le k^*_{\pool_{a,b}(u,v)}(n,\tau) \le k^*_u\!\left(n, \frac{\tau}{\lambda}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:convex} with $0 \le R_v \le 1$ gives
$\lambda R_u \le R_{\pool} \le \lambda R_u + (1-\lambda)$; translate each inequality into a
gate shift.
\end{proof}

The two gates differ by $(1-\lambda)/\lambda$, the reciprocal mass ratio.

\begin{theorem}[Mass-Share Rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
If $[(\tau-(1-\lambda))/\lambda,\ \tau/\lambda]$ lies inside a single step of the staircase
$\gamma \mapsto k^*_u(n,\gamma)$, then $k^*_{\pool_{a,b}(u,v)}(n,\tau) = k^*_u(n,\tau)$.
The required mass share is $<1$ whenever the gate is strictly interior to its step, so the
hypothesis is achievable and never vacuous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both bounds of Theorem~\ref{thm:shifted} are values of one locally constant staircase
(Theorem~\ref{thm:staircase}) at gates in a single step, hence equal, and they squeeze the
mixture's knee.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[``Starts at the easier domain's level'']
Under mass dominance the interleaved knee at context $2n$ equals $2k^*_u(n,\tau)$ up to the
one-key parity slack of Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket}, which by
Corollary~\ref{cor:parity-sharp} cannot be removed.
\end{corollary}

\section{Interleaving is pooling in doubled key units}

\begin{lemma}[Matched prefixes]\label{lem:matched}
$H_{\mix(u,v)}(2k) = H_u(k) + H_v(k)$ and $H_{\mix(u,v)}(2k+1) = H_u(k+1)+H_v(k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$: passing from $2k$ to $2k+2$ adds $u_k$ and $v_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Halving Reduction]\label{thm:halving}
$R_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,2k) = R_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(n,k)$ for all $n,k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Mixed-knee bracket]\label{thm:bracket}
With $Q = k^*_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(n,\tau)$, $\;2Q-1 \le k^*_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,\tau) \le 2Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The mixture passes at $2Q$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:halving}. If it passed at $2(Q-1)$ the pool
would pass at $Q-1$, contradicting minimality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity Law]
If $Q \ge 1$ then $k^*_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,\tau) = 2Q-1$ when
$\tau \le R_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,2Q-1)$, and $2Q$ otherwise.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Both ends are generic]
For every pair of positive profiles and every $n \ge 1$: $R_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,1) > 0$; for every
gate $0 < \tau \le R_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,1)$ the mixed knee is the odd end $2Q-1$; and at
$\tau = 1$ it is the even end $2Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:parity-sharp}
The one-key slack of Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} is irremovable: no parity-free identity
refines it.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling Law]\label{thm:doubling}
$2\Delta_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(\tau,n) \le \Delta_{\mix(u,v)}(\tau,2n) + 1$ and
$\Delta_{\mix(u,v)}(\tau,2n) \le 2\Delta_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(\tau,n) + 1$; that is,
$|\Delta_{\mix} - 2\Delta_{\pool}| \le 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} at contexts $n$ and $2n$ and subtract; monotonicity of the
knee in $n$ makes the truncated subtractions ordinary.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The doubling is not a cross-domain effect]
Interleaving $u$ with $c\,u$ ($c>0$) obeys the same bounds with
$\Delta_{\pool} = \Delta_u$, since $\pool_{1,1}(u,cu) = (1+c)u$ and retained mass is
scale-invariant. A pure increment of $4$ forces a self-interleaved increment in $[7,9]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Increment audit]
If $R_w(512,8) < \tau \le R_w(512,12)$ and $R_w(1024,16) < \tau \le R_w(1024,20)$, then
$5 \le \Delta_w(\tau,512) \le 11$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the reported ``$+8$'' is a point inside a window of width $7$ that the sampling grid
cannot resolve.

\section{Many domains, and unequal rates}

\begin{theorem}[Round-robin reduction and bracket]\label{thm:rr}
$R_{\rr_m U}(mn,mk) = R_{\poolFam_m U}(n,k)$, and with
$Q = k^*_{\poolFam_m U}(n,\tau)$,
$mQ - (m-1) \le k^*_{\rr_m U}(mn,\tau) \le mQ$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplier Law]
$\bigl|\Delta_{\rr_m U}(\tau,mn) - m \Delta_{\poolFam_m U}(\tau,n)\bigr| \le m-1$.
The case $m=2$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:doubling}; a three-domain interleaving must show a
tripled increment.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rarest-Domain Multiplier]
For the $s{:}1$ interleaving of $u$ and $v$, with $Q$ the knee of the corresponding uneven
pool at context $n$,
$(s+1)Q - s \le k^*_{s:1}((s+1)n,\tau) \le (s+1)Q$; the increment multiplier is
$s+1 = 1/\min_j p_j$, the reciprocal of the rarest mixing rate, and \emph{not} the number of
domains, which is $2$ for every $s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{prediction}
A $90{:}10$ code/prose mixture should show a tenfold, not twofold, context-doubling
increment.
\end{prediction}

\begin{theorem}[Fair-comparison dichotomy]
Fix $0<\tau<1$. If $u,v$ decay geometrically with common ratio $r<1$, then a single constant
$C$ bounds both $k^*_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,\tau)$ and $k^*_u(n,\tau)$ for all $n$. For the flat
profile used as both domains, the mixed-minus-pure excess is unbounded in $n$. There is no
middle ground: a growing excess requires a gapless spectrum.
\end{theorem}

\section{The gate staircase}

\begin{theorem}[Staircase]\label{thm:staircase}
If $R_w(n,m) < \tau \le R_w(n,m+1)$ then $k^*_w(n,\tau) = m+1$. Hence
$\gamma \mapsto k^*_w(n,\gamma)$ is a step function whose step at value $k$ is
$(R_w(n,k-1), R_w(n,k)]$, of width $\sw_w(n,k)$ for $k<n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Stability radius]
With $K = k^*_w(n,\tau) \ge 1$, every gate $\tau'$ with
$|\tau'-\tau| < \min(\tau - R_w(n,K-1),\ R_w(n,K)-\tau)$ gives $k^*_w(n,\tau') = K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
If $\tau = R_w(n,k)$ with $0<k<n$ then $k^*_w(n,\tau) = k$ while
$k^*_w(n,\tau+\varepsilon) = k+1$ for every $0 < \varepsilon \le \sw_w(n,k)$. The radius
cannot be enlarged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Step splitting]
$\sw_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,2k) + \sw_{\mix(u,v)}(2n,2k+1) = \sw_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(n,k)$, with each
mixed sub-step exactly half the pooled step when $v=u$. For an $m$-fold round robin the
sub-step at index $mk+j$ is the normalised mass of key $k$ of domain $j$, and the $m$
sub-steps sum to the pooled step.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Resolution divides by $m$]
Some sub-step is at most $1/m$ of the pooled step, and the bound is realised: an explicit
gate exists at which a perturbation of that size moves the round-robin knee by one key.
\end{corollary}

Define the \emph{density} of a protocol as (context-doubling increment) $\times$
(resolution).

\begin{theorem}[Conservation inequality]
For every family of domains the interleaved density exceeds the pooled density by at most one
pooled step: interleaving is never a measurement amplifier.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact case and unbounded loss]
When the $m$ interleaved domains are copies of one profile, the resolution is exactly $1/m$
of the pooled step and the densities agree up to $(1-1/m)$ of a pooled step; the increment
slack $m-1$ is exactly one resolution unit. Without balance, for every constant $C$ there is
a two-domain mixture (weights $1$ and $\varepsilon$) whose resolution is smaller than the
pooled step divided by $C$; unbalanced mixing strictly destroys information.
\end{theorem}

\section{Block interleaving, exactly}

\begin{theorem}[Aligned invariance]
$H_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bk) = H_u(bk)+H_v(bk)$ and
$R_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bn,2bk) = R_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(bn,bk)$: at block-aligned budgets the
block size is invisible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Quantisation only]
With $Q = k^*_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(bn,\tau)$,
$2Q - 2b < k^*_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bn,\tau) \le 2Q + 2b$. A block-size sweep should show no
systematic trend, only $\pm b$ jitter.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Intra-block master identities]
For $b\ge 1$, $0 \le r \le b$ and any $q$,
\[
H_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bq+r) = H_u(bq+r) + H_v(bq), \qquad
H_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bq+b+r) = H_u(bq+b) + H_v(bq+r).
\]
Inside a half-block only one domain accumulates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $r$, using that the key at position $2bq+i$ ($i<b$) is key $bq+i$ of the first
domain and the key at $2bq+b+i$ is key $bq+i$ of the second.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact blocked knee]
Fix $q<n$ and $1 \le r \le b$. If
\[
\frac{H_u(bq+r-1)+H_v(bq)}{H_u(bn)+H_v(bn)} < \tau \le \frac{H_u(bq+r)+H_v(bq)}{H_u(bn)+H_v(bn)},
\]
then $k^*_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bn,\tau) = 2bq+r$, and symmetrically in the second half of a
block pair. Two component-level inequalities determine the blocked knee to a single integer;
no bracket remains, and no mixed context need ever be built.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharper aligned upper bound]
With $Q = k^*_{\pool_{1,1}(u,v)}(bn,\tau) < bn$,
$k^*_{\blockmix_b(u,v)}(2bn,\tau) \le 2Q + b - (Q \bmod b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $Q = bq+s$, $s = Q \bmod b$. The second-half identity at $(q,s)$ shows the blocked
mixture already passes at budget $2bq+b+s = 2Q+b-s$, because $H_u(bq+b) \ge H_u(Q)$ and
$H_v(bq+s) = H_v(Q)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The mixing-ratio sweep}

\begin{definition}[Domination]
$u$ uniformly dominates $v$ at context $n$ if $R_v(n,k) \le R_u(n,k)$ for every $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ratio monotonicity]
Under domination, if $a_2 b_1 \le a_1 b_2$ then
$R_{\pool_{a_2,b_2}}(n,k) \le R_{\pool_{a_1,b_1}}(n,k)$ for all $k$, hence
$k^*_{\pool_{a_1,b_1}}(n,\tau) \le k^*_{\pool_{a_2,b_2}}(n,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clearing denominators reduces the claim to
$(a_1b_2-a_2b_1)\bigl(H_u(\min(k,n))H_v(n) - H_v(\min(k,n))H_u(n)\bigr) \ge 0$: the first
factor is the ratio hypothesis, the second is domination.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Predicted shape]
Under domination a mixing-ratio sweep is a monotone staircase from the easier domain's knee
to the harder domain's knee, with no overshoot and no reversal. A non-monotone measured
sweep refutes domination.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Collapse region and critical weight]
$\mathcal{C} = \{a>0 : k^*_{\pool_{a,1}(u,v)}(n,\tau) = k^*_u(n,\tau)\}$ and
$a^{*} = \inf \mathcal{C}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Single phase boundary]
Under domination $\mathcal{C}$ is upward closed; under strict gate interiority it is
non-empty with an explicit witness weight computable from component head masses. Hence the
sweep is a step function of $a$ with one identified threshold. A single balanced measurement
whose pooled knee misses the dominant knee forces $a^{*} > 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Linearity of the pass condition]
For $k \le n$ and $a>0$,
$\tau \le R_{\pool_{a,1}(u,v)}(n,k)$ iff
$\tau(aH_u(n)+H_v(n)) \le aH_u(k)+H_v(k)$. If $D = H_u(k)-\tau H_u(n) > 0$ the pass set is
the half-line $\{a \ge \pi(k)\}$ with
$\pi(k) = \bigl(\tau H_v(n) - H_v(k)\bigr)/\bigl(H_u(k)-\tau H_u(n)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Closed formula for the critical weight]
Under domination, with $K = k^*_u(n,\tau)$ and $H_u(K) - \tau H_u(n) > 0$,
\[
a^{*} = \max\left(0,\ \frac{\tau H_v(n) - H_v(K)}{H_u(K) - \tau H_u(n)}\right),
\]
and $a^{*}>0$ if and only if $H_v(K) < \tau H_v(n)$, i.e.\ exactly when the weak domain fails
the gate at the dominant knee.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Under domination the pooled knee is never below $K$, so collapse is equivalent to a single
pass at budget $K$, which is the half-line $\pi(K) \le a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $u = (10,1,1,1)$, $v = (1,1,1,1)$, $n=4$, $\tau = 0.7$: $K=1$ and
$a^{*} = (0.7\cdot 4 - 1)/(10 - 0.7\cdot 13) = 1.8/0.9 = 2$. The full sweep has knee $3$ for
$a < 8/19$, knee $2$ on $[8/19,2)$, and knee $1$ for $a \ge 2$; both kinks are values of
$\pi$. The balanced protocol $a=1$ sits strictly below $a^{*}$, which is why its knee $2$
exceeds the dominant knee $1$. A ratio sweep therefore needs no mixed measurement.
\end{example}

\section{A budget measurement is a spectral measurement}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit universal budget]
If $w_{i+1} \le r w_i$ with $0<r<1$ and $r^K \le (1-\tau)(1-r)$, then $k^*_w(n,\tau) \le K$
for every $n$. The criterion is monotone in $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Lower estimator]
An observation $k^*_w(n,\tau) > K$ refutes every candidate ratio $r_0$ with
$r_0^K \le (1-\tau)(1-r_0)$, certifying $r_0 < r$. For the interleaved protocol the threshold
doubles: a mixed knee exceeding $2K$ at context $2n$ certifies the same.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Upper estimator]
Let $w$ be nonincreasing with floor rate $q>0$ ($q w_i \le w_{i+1}$) and $n \ge 2K$. Then
$R_w(n,K) \le 1/(1+q^{2K})$, independently of $n$; hence $k^*_w(n,\tau) \le K$ certifies
$q^{2K} \le (1-\tau)/\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Monotonicity gives $H_w(K) \le Kw_0$; the floor gives $w_i \ge q^i w_0$, so the second block
of $K$ keys carries at least $Kq^{2K}w_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided bracket]
A single exact knee value $k^*_w(n,\tau) = K_0$ brackets the spectrum from both sides. On the
reported numbers: a mixed knee of $20$ at gate $0.99$ exceeds $2K$ for $K = 9$, and
$(1/2)^9 = 1/512 \le (1-0.99)(1-1/2)$, so the decay ratio satisfies $r > 1/2$; dually the
halving reduction forces the pooled knee to be at most $10$, whence $q^{20} \le 1/99$ and the
floor rate is below $4/5$. Two integers thus determine the window $(1/2,4/5)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Knee by prefix scan.} Accumulate head mass until $H \ge \tau H_w(n)$ and return
the count; $O(n)$ time. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:staircase}.

\textbf{B. Blocked-mixture knee without building the mixture.} From pure head-mass arrays,
evaluate the intra-block identities at each candidate budget and return the first that
reaches $\tau$; $O(bn)$ time, no mixed context materialised.

\textbf{C. Ratio-sweep kink prediction.} Compute $K$ by A, then $a^{*}$ by the closed
formula; $O(n)$ time. Evaluating $\pi(k)$ at every $k \le n$ recovers the whole staircase of
the sweep.

\textbf{D. Stability audit.} Report
$\min(\tau - R_w(n,K-1),\ R_w(n,K)-\tau)$ alongside every knee. If the radius is small
relative to gate uncertainty, the reported knee is a property of the gate.

\section{Discussion}

P1 is false in the strongest sense: no function of the component knees exists. P2 is possible
but not necessary; the cage permits either endpoint, and mass-share rigidity explains which
occurs. P3 is true, but the structure is arithmetic --- a mediant, a convex combination with
weight the mass share, and a staircase whose steps are individual key masses --- rather than
a novel cross-domain interaction; the doubled increment appears even when a domain is
interleaved with a rescaled copy of itself.

Ratio monotonicity, the phase boundary and the closed formula require domination; the three
witnesses show what fails without it. The rarest-domain multiplier covers rational rates with
minority share $1/(s+1)$. The spectral estimators require a geometric envelope and a floor
rate on a sorted profile.

The theory forbids: a block-size trend beyond one block; a drifting rather than kinking ratio
sweep under domination; an interleaved protocol more informative than its pooled counterpart;
and a mixed increment differing from $m$ (or $s+1$) times the pooled increment by more than
$m-1$ (or $s$) keys. Each is cheap to test.

\section{Conclusion}

``Starts low'' is mass-share rigidity: when one domain owns enough of the attention mass, the
mixture's key budget equals that domain's budget exactly. ``Rises fast'' is a change of
units: interleaving is pooling with each key split in two, so increments are multiplied by
the interleaving period. The apparent novelty of mixed-domain attention resolves into a
mediant, a convex combination and a staircase --- and becomes predictive: a tenfold increment
for a $90{:}10$ mixture, no trend in a block-size sweep, one computable kink in a ratio
sweep, and a two-sided bracket on the attention spectrum from two integers in a table.

